办」写进 todo.md\n- [x] 计算 1 加到 100，把算式过程写进 sum.txt\n\nHmm, but maybe unchecked boxes since... well after I finish them they're done. I'll mark them done after writing. Or keep simple with checked boxes since both get done in this turn.\n\nsum.txt: calculation process:\n1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 100\n配对法（高斯求和）：\n(1+100) + (2+99) + ... + (50+51)\n= 50 × 101\n= 5050\n公式：n(n+1)/2 = 100×101/2 = 5050\n\nI'll do both write calls in one block since they're independent. The files don't exist yet presumably — write tool says read existing before overwriting; but these are new files. Let me check quickly? The default fs-observation-policy requires reading before overwriting an existing file. I don't know if they exist. A quick glob could check, but honestly in an empty fresh workspace /tmp/bot-p620/work/par1-two-tasks-3 it's likely new. To be safe, I could glob for *.md and *.txt first. That adds a step but is safer. Let me do a quick glob in parallel with... no, glob result would determine writes. But risk is minimal; still, policy says read before overwrite. A single glob call \"*\" would list files. Let me do that first, then write both files in parallel.\n\nActually I can do one glob call with pattern \"*\" to see all files. Then write both. That's fine.\n\nOr I could just do the two writes — if files existed, write tool might still succeed but policy violation. Let me be safe with a glob first.\n\nThen respond with results: show contents briefly.\n\nSince the user emphasized parallel, I'll mention both were done simultaneously in one batch. Keep response concise."}}}}
